    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d693">

                <pb facs="0546" n="538" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg538"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.00111 <hi rendition="#superscript">Lic. Martin
                        Company, Glenn L./93</hi>/21: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at Hong Kong</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Donovan</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-09-22T11:00:00-04:00">September 22, 1937—11
                        a.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Telegram of August 26 and instruction of August 27,<anchor n="29"
                        xml:id="d693-f1"/> in regard to arms export license No. 2965. Glenn L.
                    Martin informs me that its mechanics in Hong Kong are to assemble the
                        planes.<anchor n="30" xml:id="d693-f2"/> One of the mechanics, Harry Roland,
                    care Pan American Airways, is stated to have in his possession a letter
                    presumably from a consular officer, informing him that he will render himself
                    liable to fine and imprisonment if he assists the Chinese Government in any way.
                    This letter appears to have raised doubts in the minds of the mechanics as to
                    whether they can legally proceed with the assembling of the planes.</p>
                <p>The Department perceives no legal or other objection to the assembling by
                    American mechanics of these planes which were legally <pb facs="0547" n="539"
                        xml:id="pg539"/> exported. Unless circumstances unknown to the Department
                    lead you to some other conclusion, you should so inform Roland.</p>
                <p>Report by telegram giving text of the letter in question.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d693-f1" n="29" xml:id="d693-f1-content">
                <p>Neither printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d693-f2" n="30" xml:id="d693-f2-content">
                <p>Three airplanes, nine machine guns, and various other items were shipped on
                    Chinese vessel, <hi rendition="#italic">Tai Yin</hi>, to the Chinese Minister of
                    Finance, Kung.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
